-- title:
--   Reduction of an adapted basis at almost all constant places
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be an algebraically closed field and $F$ a field extension of $L$ such that $F$ is finite-dimensional and separable over the intermediate field $L(f)$ generated by a given $f \in F$. Let $D$ be a divisor of $F/L$, i.e. a finitely supported integer-valued function on the places of $F/L$ (a place being a proper valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $L$ whose ring is a principal ideal ring), and suppose $D v = \max(0, -v.\mathrm{ord}\, f)$ for every place $v$, so $D$ is the pole divisor of $f$. Let $d' \in \mathbb{N}$, $y : \mathrm{Fin}\, d' \to F$ and $e : \mathrm{Fin}\, d' \to \mathbb{N}$ with $d' = [F : L(f)]$, and assume: for every $M \in \mathbb{N}$ the space $\mathcal{L}(M D) = \{z : v(z) \le \exp(M D v) \text{ for all } v\}$ is contained in the $L$-span of the monomials $f^j y_\sigma$ with $j + e_\sigma \le M$; the family $(\sigma, j) \mapsto f^j y_\sigma$, indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\, d' \times \mathbb{N}$, is $L$-linearly independent; and $y_\sigma \in \mathcal{L}(e_\sigma D)$ for each $\sigma$. Then there is a finite set $S \subseteq L$ of nonzero elements with the following property. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $L$ such that every $s \in S$ has $A$-valuation $1$, let $Fb$ be a field that is an algebra over the residue field $\kappa = \kappa(A)$, and let $R$ be a regular prolongation of $A$ to $F$ with values in $Fb$: a valuation subring $R.\mathrm{integers}$ of $F$ together with a surjective ring homomorphism $R.\mathrm{residue}$ onto $Fb$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal, contracting to $A$ on $L$, compatible with the residue map of $A$, and such that every nonzero element of $F$ has an $L$-multiple lying in $R.\mathrm{integers}$ with nonzero residue. Assume $f \in R.\mathrm{integers}$, that $\bar f = R.\mathrm{residue}(f)$ is transcendental over $\kappa$, and that $[Fb : \kappa(\bar f)] = [F : L(f)]$. Then every $y_\sigma$ lies in $R.\mathrm{integers}$ and, writing $\bar y_\sigma$ for its residue, for every divisor $\bar D$ of $Fb/\kappa$ with $\bar D w = \max(0, -w.\mathrm{ord}\, \bar f)$ for all places $w$ one has: $\mathcal{L}(M \bar D)$ lies in the $\kappa$-span of the $\bar f^{\,j} \bar y_\sigma$ with $j + e_\sigma \le M$, for every $M$; the family $(\sigma, j) \mapsto \bar f^{\,j} \bar y_\sigma$ is $\kappa$-linearly independent; and $\bar y_\sigma \in \mathcal{L}(e_\sigma \bar D)$ for each $\sigma$.
--
--   This is the reduction step in Deuring's theory of reduction of a one-variable function field modulo a place of its field of constants: an adapted basis for the pole divisor of a separating generator $f$ reduces, at all but finitely many places of $L$, to an adapted basis with the same exponents for the pole divisor of $\bar f$. It is used in [`AlgebraicCurve.RegularProlongation.exists_finset_forall_valuation_eq_one_forall_exists_forall_ell_nsmul_le`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.RegularProlongation.exists_finset_forall_valuation_eq_one_forall_exists_forall_ell_nsmul_le) to compare the dimensions $\ell(M \bar D)$ and $\ell(M D)$, and hence to control the genus under reduction. The hypotheses that the reduction of $f$ is transcendental and that the residual degree $[Fb : \kappa(\bar f)]$ equals $[F : L(f)]$ cannot be dropped: without them the spanning assertion may fail.
